WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:07.499
This is Chapter 6: private
applications and administrative paths.

3
00:00:07.499 --> 00:00:10.375
A VPN gives a device an address range.

4
00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:13.565
Private Access gives a
group one application,

5
00:00:13.565 --> 00:00:16.838
on its ports, behind its
own Conditional Access.

6
00:00:16.838 --> 00:00:19.301
The code checks that
each app is that narrow,

7
00:00:19.301 --> 00:00:23.612
and that administrative paths ask for
phishing-resistant authentication.

8
00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:26.385
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved,

9
00:00:26.385 --> 00:00:30.200
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:34.788
Why narrow?

11
00:00:34.788 --> 00:00:38.169
Because a compromised user
or device on a VPN address

12
00:00:38.169 --> 00:00:42.012
pool can move laterally across
everything the pool reaches.

13
00:00:42.012 --> 00:00:46.388
Private Access replaces the range
with one application for one group,

14
00:00:46.388 --> 00:00:48.726
behind that application's own policy.

15
00:00:48.726 --> 00:00:51.794
So the goal: show,
application by application,

16
00:00:51.794 --> 00:00:55.858
that each reaches only its host
and ports, only for its group,

17
00:00:55.858 --> 00:01:01.333
under a policy that excludes emergency
access; that administrative RDP and SSH

18
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:04.485
require phishing-resistant authentication;

19
00:01:04.485 --> 00:01:07.388
and that connectors survive a patch night.

20
00:01:07.388 --> 00:01:10.699
It's for the developers, the
server and application owners,

21
00:01:10.699 --> 00:01:12.910
and the privileged access owner.

22
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.412
Learn's Private Access tutorial creates
an application from the custom template,

23
00:01:21.412 --> 00:01:24.766
marks it a Private Access app,
binds it to a connector group,

24
00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:28.260
adds its segments and assigns a
group with the app's user role.

25
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.687
Segments may not overlap between
per-app applications; a per-app segment

26
00:01:32.687 --> 00:01:37.335
overrides Quick Access; and clients can
take fifteen minutes to see a change.

27
00:01:37.335 --> 00:01:40.373
Connectors are read with
a write-capable permission

28
00:01:40.373 --> 00:01:43.135
and report active or
inactive, and their version.

29
00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:46.554
Learn's Zero Trust recommendations
ask for two healthy,

30
00:01:46.554 --> 00:01:51.460
current connectors per group, least-privilege
segments and every app assigned.

31
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.900
And for domain controllers,
the sensor starts in audit,

32
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.480
with application service
names before the DC's own.

33
00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.455
What does it leave to you?

34
00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:05.097
The requests create one app,

35
00:02:05.097 --> 00:02:09.881
but nothing reads all of them back with
their segments and assignments, so the kit

36
00:02:09.881 --> 00:02:14.380
has a read for that, marked unverified,
and the lab compares it with the portal.

37
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.767
A group with one inactive
connector looks fine in a list,

38
00:02:17.767 --> 00:02:19.755
so the kit counts active members.

39
00:02:19.755 --> 00:02:25.805
A wide range on every port is the VPN
again, so wide segments and overlaps fail.

40
00:02:25.805 --> 00:02:29.318
A person assigned directly
escapes the access review.

41
00:02:29.318 --> 00:02:34.925
Plain MFA on RDP passes a casual
look, so any app covering port 22

42
00:02:34.925 --> 00:02:41.180
or 3389 needs the phishing-resistant
strength and a compliant device.

43
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.050
And Quick Access is
tracked until it's empty.

44
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.800
Nine checks.

45
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.050
Two or more active connectors per
group, each at the minimum version.

46
00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.138
Segments left in Quick Access,
a warning until there are none.

47
00:02:57.138 --> 00:03:02.725
Each segment a host or a narrow range,
on named ports, with no overlaps.

48
00:03:02.725 --> 00:03:04.112
Group assignments only.

49
00:03:04.112 --> 00:03:08.026
A policy that targets the app
and excludes the emergency group.

50
00:03:08.026 --> 00:03:10.212
RDP and SSH apps that require the

51
00:03:10.212 --> 00:03:13.775
phishing-resistant strength
and a compliant device.

52
00:03:13.775 --> 00:03:18.525
And the last day's private sessions by
destination, to compare with the assignments.

53
00:03:18.525 --> 00:03:22.443
The administrative policy is a
reviewed file, the guide's own;

54
00:03:22.443 --> 00:03:26.160
when the tenant's copy drifts,
the plan brings it back.

55
00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:30.860
Who owns what?

56
00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:35.323
Application owners own their app's
assignment and review it each quarter.

57
00:03:35.323 --> 00:03:40.711
The platform owner for servers keeps two
healthy, current connectors in every group.

58
00:03:40.711 --> 00:03:45.351
The privileged access owner owns the
administrative apps and the domain controller

59
00:03:45.351 --> 00:03:48.618
sensor, and that's where Building
Privileged Access comes in,

60
00:03:48.618 --> 00:03:50.710
for the privileged groups behind them.

61
00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:55.264
For permissions: a write-capable delegated
permission for the connector reads,

62
00:03:55.264 --> 00:03:58.506
an application read for the
apps, which isn't verified yet,

63
00:03:58.506 --> 00:04:03.570
the policy permissions, and three
administrator roles activated just for the lab.

64
00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:11.220
Lab 1, after listing the reads
and collecting the pilot.

65
00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:14.307
Step 3 reads the
connectors group by group.

66
00:04:14.307 --> 00:04:17.657
Headquarters has two active
connectors on the current version.

67
00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:22.307
The cloud group has one active and one
inactive, and the active one is old.

68
00:04:22.307 --> 00:04:24.095
Step 4 runs the checks.

69
00:04:24.095 --> 00:04:25.945
Both connector problems fail.

70
00:04:25.945 --> 00:04:30.633
The Finance app is a slash sixteen
on every port, assigned to a person,

71
00:04:30.633 --> 00:04:35.070
under an enforced policy that
doesn't exclude emergency access.

72
00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:39.470
Because every port includes RDP,
it's an administrative path too.

73
00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.758
The RDP app asks only for MFA.

74
00:04:42.758 --> 00:04:45.800
And Quick Access still holds two segments.

75
00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:53.800
Step 5 plans the pilot and reads
the administrative policy's fix.

76
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.261
The plan has two Graph steps: the
internet profile off, and a patch for

77
00:04:58.261 --> 00:05:02.962
the RDP policy, because the tenant's
copy differs from the reviewed file.

78
00:05:02.962 --> 00:05:08.222
The body of that patch sets the built-in
phishing-resistant authentication strength,

79
00:05:08.222 --> 00:05:10.963
the one ending in four,
and a compliant device.

80
00:05:10.963 --> 00:05:13.862
That's the guide's Step
10 policy, as written.

81
00:05:13.862 --> 00:05:18.670
Nothing is sent: it's a dry run,
and fixture mode never applies.

82
00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.982
Step 6 checks the tenant two weeks later.

83
00:05:24.982 --> 00:05:28.613
Finance is one host on port
443 for the pilot group,

84
00:05:28.613 --> 00:05:31.182
with the emergency group excluded.

85
00:05:31.182 --> 00:05:33.894
Both cloud connectors
are active and current.

86
00:05:33.894 --> 00:05:37.857
The RDP policy has its strength
and a compliant device.

87
00:05:37.857 --> 00:05:39.558
And Quick Access is empty.

88
00:05:39.558 --> 00:05:40.945
Every check passes.

89
00:05:40.945 --> 00:05:45.307
Step 7 runs this chapter's
tests: four passed, none failed.

90
00:05:45.307 --> 00:05:47.510
Then delete the output folder.

91
00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:56.491
Lab 2 needs a test tenant with Private
Access licenses, a connector group with

92
00:05:56.491 --> 00:06:01.723
two connectors on Windows Server 2019 or
later, a test server and a pilot group.

93
00:06:01.723 --> 00:06:06.025
Publish one app with the guide's
own requests, named so the kit's

94
00:06:06.025 --> 00:06:10.497
reviewed policy file finds it, one
host on 443, for the pilot group.

95
00:06:10.497 --> 00:06:14.480
Collect and check, comparing the
app read with the portal first;

96
00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.773
the policy check fails,
because there's no policy yet.

97
00:06:17.773 --> 00:06:20.660
Plan and apply its policy in report-only.

98
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.623
Then validate: from a device
off the corporate network,

99
00:06:24.623 --> 00:06:28.322
the host is reachable on
443 and not on another port.

100
00:06:28.322 --> 00:06:31.847
Back it out by deleting the
policy, then the application.

101
00:06:31.847 --> 00:06:34.210
The authors haven't run this lab.

102
00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:41.258
Narrow apps for one group
support access enforcement,

103
00:06:41.258 --> 00:06:44.410
least privilege and managed
access control points.

104
00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.323
Resilient connectors
support remote access.

105
00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:52.885
Phishing-resistant administrative paths
support multifactor authentication and

106
00:06:52.885 --> 00:06:58.839
logging privileged functions; the guide
lists OMB M-22-09's phishing-resistant

107
00:06:58.839 --> 00:07:03.697
MFA and VPN-free application access
among the requirements this supports.

108
00:07:03.697 --> 00:07:05.861
The assessor decides the controls.

109
00:07:05.861 --> 00:07:10.766
The dangers: a wide segment, a group
with one connector, plain MFA on RDP,

110
00:07:10.766 --> 00:07:15.672
Quick Access left as a range, and
protecting a domain controller's own

111
00:07:15.672 --> 00:07:20.922
service name before the applications',
which can lock administrators out.

112
00:07:20.922 --> 00:07:25.848
You're done when every group, every app
and every administrative path passes.

113
00:07:25.848 --> 00:07:27.690
Next, Chapter 7.
